      },
      {
        "content": [
          {
            "text": "Method",
            "type": "text"
          }
        ],
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        }
      },
      {
        "content": [
          {
            "content": [
              {
                "content": [
                  {
                    "text": "Whisk the rice flour, coconut milk, water, and turmeric together until smooth. Stir in the sliced spring onion. Leave the batter to rest for 30 minutes.",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ],
                "type": "paragraph"
              }
            ],
            "type": "listItem"
          },
          {
            "content": [
              {
                "content": [
                  {
                    "text": "Mix the dipping sauce by combining the fish sauce, lime juice, sugar, and 4 tbsp of water. Stir until the sugar dissolves. Add a sliced chilli and some minced garlic if you have them.",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ],
                "type": "paragraph"
              }
            ],
            "type": "listItem"
          },
          {
            "content": [
              {
                "content": [
                  {
                    "text": "Heat 1 tbsp of oil in a large frying pan over high heat. Add a few slices of pork belly and some shallots. Stir-fry for 2 minutes.",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ],
                "type": "paragraph"
              }
            ],
            "type": "listItem"
          },
          {
            "content": [
              {
                "content": [
                  {
                    "text": "Add a few prawns and cook for 1 minute until starting to turn pink. Push everything to one side of the pan.",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ],
                "type": "paragraph"
              }
            ],
            "type": "listItem"
          },
          {
            "content": [
              {
                "content": [
                  {
                    "text": "Pour a ladleful of batter into the empty side of the pan and swirl to cover the base. Listen for the loud sizzle.",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ],
                "type": "paragraph"
              }
            ],
            "type": "listItem"
          },
          {
            "content": [
              {
                "content": [
                  {
                    "text": "Scatter a handful of bean sprouts over the filling side. Cover with a lid and cook for 3 minutes until the edges of the crepe are crispy and the top is set.",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ],
                "type": "paragraph"
              }
            ],
            "type": "listItem"
          },
          {
            "content": [
              {
                "content": [
                  {
                    "text": "Remove the lid and fold the crepe in half over the filling. Slide onto a plate and repeat with the remaining batter and filling.",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ],
                "type": "paragraph"
              }
            ],
            "type": "listItem"
          },
          {
            "content": [
              {
                "content": [
                  {
                    "text": "Serve with lettuce leaves, fresh mint, coriander, and the dipping sauce. To eat, tear off a piece of crepe and wrap it in a lettuce leaf with herbs. ",
                    "type": "text"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Cuốn rau sống ",
                    "type": "text",
                    "marks": [
                      {
                        "type": "glossaryTooltip",
                        "attrs": {
                          "termSlug": "lettuce-wrap-viet"
                        }
                      }
                    ]
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ". rolling in leaves . is the traditional way to eat this dish.",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ],
                "type": "paragraph"
              }
            ],
            "type": "listItem"
          }
        ],
        "type": "orderedList"
      },
      {
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "fix": "Heat the pan until smoking before adding oil, then add the batter. Add an extra drizzle of oil around the edge of the crepe.",
              "cause": "The pan was not hot enough or did not have enough oil.",
              "symptom": "The crepe sticks to the pan and tears."
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "fix": "Thin the batter with a little more water until it is the consistency of single cream. Keep the heat high throughout cooking.",
              "cause": "The batter is too thick or the heat is too low.",
              "symptom": "The crepe is soft and floppy instead of crispy."
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "fix": "Always cook the pork and prawns first, then add the batter. The filling only needs to heat through inside the crepe.",
              "cause": "The filling was added to the batter at the same time instead of cooking first.",
              "symptom": "The filling is undercooked inside the crepe."
            }
          }
        ],
        "type": "troubleshooter"
      },
      {
        "content": [
          {
            "text": "Where this dish lives",
            "type": "text"
          }
        ],
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        }
      },
      {
        "content": [
          {
            "text": "Bánh xèo is eaten all over Vietnam, but it is especially popular in the south and centre of the country. ",
            "type": "text"
          },
          {
            "text": "The central version from Da Nang and Quang Nam is smaller and uses different herbs. ",
            "type": "text"
          },
          {
            "text": "Street vendors cook the crepes to order in front of customers on small gas stoves. ",
            "type": "text"
          },
          {
            "text": "The dish is eaten wrapped in leaves, which makes it a hands-on, communal meal. ",
            "type": "text"
          },
          {
            "text": "It is popular at celebrations and family gatherings as well as every day street food.",
            "type": "text"
          }
        ],
        "type": "paragraph"
      }
    ],
    "type": "doc"
  },
  "glossaryTerms": [
    {
      "definition": "A thin batter made from rice flour, coconut milk, and turmeric. When poured into a very hot pan, it sizzles loudly and sets into a crispy, golden crepe.",
      "term": "Bánh xèo batter",
      "slug": "banh-xeo-batter"
    },
    {
      "definition": "The Vietnamese technique of wrapping food in a large lettuce leaf with herbs. The bundle is then dipped into sauce and eaten in one bite.",
      "term": "Cuốn rau sống",
      "slug": "lettuce-wrap-viet"
    }
  ],
